From c9e420d97d21ce5e9e5a4578540a5897301805c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:44:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add sync: trade the store with other devices through a drop
 zone

A drop zone is a directory every device can reach and nobody has to trust:
a local path, or sftp://user@host[:port][/dir]. Each device publishes one
file there, <device>.bundle.gpg: a git bundle of its whole history under the
single reference refs/pass-sync/<device>, signed with the device's OpenPGP
key (SHA-512) and encrypted to every device.

    pass sync setup device-name drop-zone own-key-fingerprint
    pass sync peer [--remove] device-name [key-fingerprint]
    pass sync push | fetch [device-name...]
    pass sync log | diff | merge device-name

This side is the owner of the store. It takes a file only when it decrypts
to no more than 256 MiB, carries one valid signature by the key configured
for that device (not a SHA-1 one), and holds that device's reference alone
with its full history; an older file served again is ignored. It merges a
device's commits only as a fast-forward, only when each is signed by that
device or by this one, and only when they change nothing but entries: no
.gpg-id, nothing under .extensions/, no symlink.

The settings live in the store's git configuration (pass-sync.*).
PASSWORD_STORE_GPG_OPTS applies to the gpg calls, as elsewhere.

Also exit quietly when whoever reads the output goes away (| grep -q),
instead of a BrokenPipeError traceback.

Tests: u0008-sync-tests.sh, the other device played with git and gpg alone.
